 Develops the knight backward but doesn't seem to accomplish much.\n\nLooking more carefully at the board, I notice White's rook is on h2, and my knight on g4 can capture it with N: g4-h2. This would win a full rook (worth about 5 points of material), which is a decisive advantage.\n\nReason: Capturing the rook on h2 with my knight wins major material (a full rook). Even if White recaptures the knight, I'm still up the exchange (rook for knight = about 2 points of material). Given that winning material is one of the most fundamental principles in chess, and a rook is such a valuable piece, this move gives me a clear path to victory. Material advantage of this magnitude usually converts to a win with proper technique.\n\nAnswer: N: g4-h2"}
