White's advanced king position, so removing or displacing it could weaken White's king safety.\n\nReason: Moving the rook to e8 attacks the bishop on e6, which is a key piece supporting White's dangerously advanced king on e5. This forcing move creates immediate tactical pressure and limits White's options. If the bishop moves, it may expose White's king to further attacks. This is more concrete than quiet developing moves and capitalizes on White's overextended position.\n\nAnswer: R: f8-e8"}
