\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |KWF1| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, B: e6-d5, B: e6-c4, B: e6-b3, B: e6-a2, B: e6-f5, B: e6-g4, B: e6-h3, B: e6-d7, B: e6-c8, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, R: g2-f2, R: g2-e2, R: g2-d2, R: g2-c2, R: g2-b2, R: g2-a2, R: g2-g1, R: g2-g3, R: g2-g4, R: g2-g5, R: g2-g6, R: g2-h2, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, K: g8-f8, K: g8-h8, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black in an endgame position with King on g8, Bishop on e6, Rook on g2, and pawns on a7, b7, f7, g7, h7. White has only King on h1 and two pawns on a5 and b4. This is a winning position for Black with significant material advantage (Rook + Bishop + 5 pawns vs 2 pawns).\n\nEvaluation: \n1. **R: g2-g1+**: This delivers check to the White King on h1, forcing it to move to h2. This is the most forcing move and restricts White's king mobility immediately.\n\n2. **R: g2-h2+**: Also delivers check, but the king can't escape to h2 since the rook would be there. This would actually be checkmate if legal, but let me verify - the king on h1 attacked by rook on h2, cannot move to g1 (controlled by rook), cannot move to g2 (controlled by rook), cannot move to h2 (occupied by rook). Wait, this needs careful analysis.\n\n3. **B: e6-f5 or other bishop moves**: Developing the bishop to better squares but less forcing than rook checks.\n\n4. **Pawn advances**: Pushing pawns forward to promote eventually, but slower than direct king attacks.\n\nLet me reconsider R: g2-h2. After this move:\n- White King on h1 is attacked by Black Rook on h2\n- King cannot stay on h1 (in check)\n- King cannot move to g1 (attacked by Rook on h2)  \n- King cannot move to g2 (attacked by Rook on h2)\n- King cannot capture on h2 (would still need to verify if protected)\n- This appears to be checkmate!\n\nReason: R: g2-h2 delivers checkmate. The White King on h1 has no escape squares: g1 and g2 are controlled by the rook on h2, and h2 is occupied by the rook itself. The king cannot capture the rook as it would still be moving into check from the rook's control. This is immediate checkmate and wins the game.\n\nAnswer: R: g2-h2"}
